60,3,1,talk,Mio,,"@George, @Mizuki, look at A and B. They both need field crews and facility staff. I'm voting against them! I can't spend my whole year on LinkedIn!","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,George,,"I agree. A and B are exactly the kind of labor-heavy models that led to our previous write-off. C and D are the ""boring"" tools that actually keep their customers.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,May,,"C and D are great for retention, but they're software. If they hit a rough patch, we can't borrow against their assets. However, A has physical facilities... that's something we can actually use for a credit line.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Rin,,"I hate the ""trust me"" dashboards of C and D! But A has a sorting facility—that's a real thing! I can check the boxes! But wait, will that make the audit take six weeks?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, @Rin, don't be fooled by the assets. A and B will be nightmares. B has sensors in fields—think of the delivery delays. A has facilities—think of the setup time.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""May"",""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Mio,,"I'm leaning toward D. Clinic Scheduler sounds like it has a very specific, manageable user base. It's software, so no field crews for me to find!","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,George,,"D is the strongest. Independent clinics are exactly the kind of ""independent practices"" that showed high retention in my research. It's a stable, low-overhead bet.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,May,,"I'm still worried about the cash. But if we choose D, we avoid the ""follow-on call"" trap that the hardware companies fall into. Maybe the lower risk is better than the borrowing power.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Rin,,"If we pick D, can we make them give us a way to check the numbers independently? I don't want to spend my fortnight guessing if the dashboard is lying!","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Rin, that's a fair request. But look at D versus C. C deals with banks. My research showed that security reviews for companies dealing with banks take three quarters. D is much faster.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Mio,,"Yes! D is the winner for me. No field crews, no bank-level security delays, and it's a boring tool that works. I'm so happy I won't have to recruit for a warehouse!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,George,,I'm in agreement. D aligns with the historical data on retention and avoids the operational pitfalls of the labor-heavy models. It's the most reliable choice.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,May,,"Fine, you've convinced me. I'll trade the credit line security for a company that won't bleed our reserves dry with unexpected equipment costs. D it is.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Rin,,"I guess I'll have to live with the software dashboards for now. But I'm voting for D because I really, really don't want that six-week audit delay!","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"It's the only logical choice. C is a security nightmare, and A and B are deployment disasters waiting to happen. D is the only one that can actually launch on time.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
